    {
      "id": "titus-andronicus/sceneIII_34.2/p@87158",
      "type": "speech",
      "tag": "p",
      "classes": [
        "drama"
      ],
      "text": "MARCUS.\nFie, brother, fie! Teach her not thus to lay\nSuch violent hands upon her tender life.",
      "separatorAfter": "\n\n",
      "spans": [],
      "lines": [
        {
          "id": "titus-andronicus/sceneIII_34.2/p@87158/line-1",
          "start": 0,
          "end": 7,
          "sourceLineNumber": null
        },
        {
          "id": "titus-andronicus/sceneIII_34.2/p@87158/line-2",
          "start": 8,
          "end": 52,
          "sourceLineNumber": null
        },
        {
          "id": "titus-andronicus/sceneIII_34.2/p@87158/line-3",
          "start": 53,
          "end": 93,
          "sourceLineNumber": null
        }
      ],
      "sourceRef": {
        "start": 6028517,
        "end": 6028645,
        "line": 224413,
        "column": 1,
        "path": "chap34/p@87158",
        "anchor": "sceneIII_34.2"
      },
      "speakerLabel": "MARCUS.",
      "attribution": {
        "status": "explicit",
        "cueStart": 0,
        "cueEnd": 7
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "titus-andronicus/sceneIII_34.2/p@87290",
      "type": "speech",
      "tag": "p",
      "classes": [
        "drama"
      ],
      "text": "TITUS.\nHow now! Has sorrow made thee dote already?\nWhy, Marcus, no man should be mad but I.\nWhat violent hands can she lay on her life?\nAh, wherefore dost thou urge the name of hands,\nTo bid Æneas tell the tale twice o’er\nHow Troy was burnt and he made miserable?\nO, handle not the theme, to talk of hands,\nLest we remember still that we have none.\nFie, fie, how frantically I square my talk,\nAs if we should forget we had no hands,\nIf Marcus did not name the word of hands!\nCome, let’s fall to; and, gentle girl, eat this.\nHere is no drink! Hark, Marcus, what she says;\nI can interpret all her martyred signs.\nShe says she drinks no other drink but tears,\nBrewed with her sorrow, meshed upon her cheeks.\nSpeechless complainer, I will learn thy thought;\nIn thy dumb action will I be as perfect\nAs begging hermits in their holy prayers.\nThou shalt not sigh, nor hold thy stumps to heaven,\nNor wink, nor nod, nor kneel, nor make a sign,\nBut I of these will wrest an alphabet,\nAnd by still practice learn to know thy meaning.",
      "separatorAfter": "\n\n",
      "spans": [],
